cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  A Universal Cryptographic Sealing Primitive for Digital File
  Provenance in the Age of Generative AI
message: >-
  If you refer to this work, please cite the paper. Note that the
  system name "SEAL" is provisional and collides with Microsoft SEAL.
type: generic
authors:
  - family-names: Shemesh
    given-names: Ariel
    orcid: null
abstract: >-
  A position and architecture paper on a trustless anchor for digital
  file provenance. It makes three claims: interval timestamp semantics
  whose anti-backdating bound terminates in Bitcoin via OpenTimestamps
  and therefore holds even against the system's own sequencer; a
  verifiable soft-binding resolution registry built in the
  Certificate-Transparency pattern, whose positive matches carry
  proofs and whose completeness is priced as a stated k-of-n indexer
  quorum; and longevity engineering with confidential sealing, where
  salted commitments leak nothing on-chain and epochal renewal carries
  a seal across a hash-function break. It explicitly does not claim
  the hard and soft binding of what, who and when, which belongs to
  C2PA and JPEG Trust (ISO/IEC 21617-1:2025). The evaluation plan in
  section 9 is committed, not executed. A complete reference package
  accompanies the paper so every reported figure can be regenerated.
version: "2.0"
date-released: "2026-06-11"
url: "https://github.com/ArielShemesh1999/seal-provenance"
keywords:
  - content provenance
  - digital signatures
  - document authenticity
  - blockchain anchoring
  - certificate transparency
  - C2PA
  - post-quantum cryptography
  - generative AI
